### What happens when execute() throws an unchecked exception?

When a task submitted via `pool.execute(runnable)` throws an unchecked exception, the exception propagates to the thread's `UncaughtExceptionHandler`. The worker thread dies, and the pool creates a replacement. If no custom handler is set, the default handler prints the stack trace to stderr.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ExecuteExceptionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

        // Set a default handler to catch exceptions from execute()
        Thread.setDefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler((thread, ex) -> {
            System.out.println("[ALERT] Thread " + thread.getName()
                + " crashed: " + ex.getMessage());
        });

        pool.execute(() -> {
            throw new RuntimeException("Settlement TRD-001 crashed!");
            // Worker thread dies, pool creates replacement
        });

        Thread.sleep(200);
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
[ALERT] Thread pool-1-thread-1 crashed: Settlement TRD-001 crashed!
```

### What happens when submit() throws an unchecked exception?

When a task submitted via `pool.submit()` throws an exception, the exception is captured inside the `Future`. The worker thread does not die. The exception surfaces only when `future.get()` is called, wrapped in an `ExecutionException`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class SubmitExceptionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

        Future<?> future = pool.submit(() -> {
            throw new RuntimeException("Settlement TRD-002 crashed!");
            // Exception sealed in Future. Worker thread survives.
        });

        try {
            future.get(); // THIS is where the exception surfaces
        } catch (ExecutionException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getCause().getMessage());
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
Caught: Settlement TRD-002 crashed!
```

### Why are submit() exceptions dangerous in production?

If you call `submit()` but never call `get()` on the returned Future, the exception is silently swallowed. No stack trace is printed, no log is written, and no alert fires. This is one of the most dangerous concurrency pitfalls in Java.

```java
// WRONG: exception silently lost!
pool.submit(() -> {
    throw new RuntimeException("200 settlements failed!");
});
// Nobody ever calls get() on this Future
// Exception vanishes. No log. No alert. Silent data loss.

// CORRECT: always check the Future
Future<?> f = pool.submit(() -> settleAllTrades());
try {
    f.get();
} catch (ExecutionException e) {
    log.error("Settlement batch failed", e.getCause());
    alertOps("Settlement failure: " + e.getCause().getMessage());
} catch (InterruptedException e) {
    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
}
```

### How does afterExecute() catch all exceptions?

Override `ThreadPoolExecutor.afterExecute(Runnable r, Throwable t)` to log every exception from every task, regardless of whether it was submitted via execute() or submit().

For execute() tasks, the throwable parameter is the exception itself. For submit() tasks, the throwable is null because the exception is inside the Future. You must cast the Runnable to Future and call get() to extract it.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class SafeThreadPool extends ThreadPoolExecutor {

    public SafeThreadPool(int poolSize) {
        super(poolSize, poolSize, 0L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
              new LinkedBlockingQueue<>());
    }

    @Override
    protected void afterExecute(Runnable r, Throwable t) {
        super.afterExecute(r, t);

        // Case 1: execute() path, exception in throwable
        if (t != null) {
            System.out.println("[afterExecute] execute() error: " + t.getMessage());
        }

        // Case 2: submit() path, exception inside Future
        if (t == null && r instanceof Future<?> f) {
            try {
                if (f.isDone()) {
                    f.get(); // extracts exception from Future
                }
            } catch (ExecutionException e) {
                System.out.println("[afterExecute] submit() error: "
                    + e.getCause().getMessage());
            } catch (InterruptedException | CancellationException e) {
                // Interrupted or cancelled: not an error
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        SafeThreadPool pool = new SafeThreadPool(2);

        pool.submit(() -> {
            throw new RuntimeException("Settlement crashed!");
        });

        Thread.sleep(200);
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
[afterExecute] submit() error: Settlement crashed!
```

### How do you set a custom UncaughtExceptionHandler?

You can set a handler per thread via a custom ThreadFactory, or set a JVM-wide default handler.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class CustomHandlerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        // Per-thread handler via ThreadFactory
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2, r -> {
            Thread t = new Thread(r, "settlement-worker");
            t.setUncaughtExceptionHandler((thread, ex) -> {
                System.out.println("[HANDLER] " + thread.getName()
                    + " failed: " + ex.getMessage());
            });
            return t;
        });

        pool.execute(() -> {
            throw new RuntimeException("TRD-003 settlement error");
        });

        Thread.sleep(200);
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
[HANDLER] settlement-worker failed: TRD-003 settlement error
```

### What is the correct production pattern for exception safety?

Use a three-layer defense: always call `future.get()`, override `afterExecute()` as a safety net, and set a default `UncaughtExceptionHandler` as a last resort.

```java
// Layer 1: always call get()
Future<?> f = pool.submit(() -> processPayment());
try { f.get(); } catch (ExecutionException e) { handleError(e.getCause()); }

// Layer 2: override afterExecute() in a custom pool
// (catches anything Layer 1 misses)

// Layer 3: default handler for raw Thread exceptions
Thread.setDefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler((t, ex) -> {
    log.error("Uncaught in {}: {}", t.getName(), ex.getMessage(), ex);
});
```

### What are common mistakes with exception handling in thread pools?

```java
// WRONG: submit() without get() -- exception lost
pool.submit(() -> { throw new RuntimeException("Lost!"); });

// WRONG: catching Exception inside task and swallowing
pool.submit(() -> {
    try { riskyOperation(); }
    catch (Exception e) { /* swallowed! */ }
});

// CORRECT: let exception propagate, handle at Future level
Future<?> f = pool.submit(() -> riskyOperation());
try { f.get(); }
catch (ExecutionException e) {
    log.error("Task failed", e.getCause());
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does the worker thread die when submit() throws? No, it survives.
- Does the worker thread die when execute() throws? Yes, but the pool replaces it.
- How do you detect silent exception loss in production? Use afterExecute() or always call get().
- What is the difference between ExecutionException and CompletionException? ExecutionException wraps exceptions from Future.get(), CompletionException wraps exceptions from CompletableFuture.join().
